            Next R%
            '   All but First 3:  Expenditure Components - Total
            '   ------------------------------------------------

            '   General behavioral assumptions on expenditure components:
            '       Government (above) increases consumption and transfers with GDPPC,
            '           and even a little faster over time - curve shift(Wagner's law)
            '       Government is subject to need to balance revenues/expenditures and
            '           to feedback on both from imbalance

            '       Firms (above) pass on all after tax income to households

            '       Households gradually decrease consumption share of after tax income as get richer
            '           The curves in 1960, 1980, and 2000 are almost identical
            '       Households tend to smooth consumption over time, as possible, allowing more swing
            '           in savings as residual

            '       Investment is affected by all of above (tends to be squeezed by increased
            '           govt consumption and boosted by decreased household consumption)
            '       Estimations support clearly increased govt consumption and decreased household;
            '           investment pattern is much less clear across countries and time;
            '           much lower correlations with GDP/capita at PPP

            '       In terms of maintaining physical balances, we allow govt dynamics,
            '           household dynamics and trade dynamics to function, leaving investment as residual,
            '           making preliminary calculation that is response to above dynamics
            '           and then allowing prices to signal stock imbalances and therefore investment swing

            'jrs 2014/09/16
            Dim CNonRetire As Single, Cremaining As Single, inversecalc As Single, CPar1 As Single, CPar2 As Single, CHold As Single
